LeetCode 443 – String Compression

Problem

Compress consecutive repeated characters in a character array.

Example:
['a','a','b','b','c','c','c']

becomes:

['a','2','b','2','c','3']

Return the length of the compressed part: 6.

Approach

Use two pointers:

i scans the array and finds groups of equal characters.

index tells us where to write the compressed result.

Example

For:
a a a b b c

The first group is a a a, so:
count = 3

Write:
a 3

Then move to the next group.

Code

class Solution {
public:
    int compress(vector<char>& chars) {
        int index = 0;
        int n = chars.size();

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            char ch = chars[i];
            int count = 0;

            while(i < n && chars[i] == ch) {
                count++;
                i++;
            }

            chars[index++] = ch;

            if(count > 1) {
                string str = to_string(count);

                for(char digit : str) {
                    chars[index++] = digit;
                }
            }

            i--;
        }

        return index;
    }
};

Key Concepts

count stores how many consecutive copies of a character exist.

to_string(count) converts a number into characters.

If count = 12, the string is "12", so the loop stores '1' and '2' separately in vector<char>.

index is the write pointer.

i is the scanning pointer.

i-- prevents the outer for loop from skipping the first character of the next group.

The original array is modified in-place.

Complexity

Time: O(n)

Extra Space: O(log n) for the temporary count string in the worst case.
